# HostTracker Provider

Manage [HostTracker](https://www.host-tracker.com) monitoring as code. Monitors declared in Terraform are created,
changed and removed through the HostTracker **API v2**, the same surface the web app and the SDKs use.

## Authentication

Mint a token in the web app under **Integrations → API**
(<https://www.host-tracker.com/integrations/api>), carrying the scopes the configuration needs: `monitor:read` and
`monitor:write` for the monitor resource, plus `account:read` for the `hosttracker_account` data source. A token may
also carry an IP allow-list and a self-cap.

Put it in the `HT_TOKEN` environment variable rather than in the provider block: a token written into the
configuration ends up in version control, and in the state file as well. A token cannot be revoked before it
expires - only an account-wide API disable kills every token at once.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

```hcl
resource "hosttracker_monitor" "marketing" {
  type     = "http"
  url      = "https://example.com/"
  name     = "Marketing site"
  interval = 300

  locations = {
    pools = ["allworld"]
  }

  settings = {
    http = {
      keywords        = "Sign in"
      follow_redirect = true
    }
  }
}
```

## Rate limits

Terraform's default parallelism of 10 is comfortable under a paid plan's quota. On a trial token (10 reads and 5
writes a minute) run `terraform apply -parallelism=3`. The provider retries a `429 rate_limited` honouring the
API's `Retry-After`, but a whole plan applied at once can still exhaust the window.

